'' =============================================================================
'' Throughput / stream-integrity stress — stress01_stream
''
'' Purpose: let the HOST LOG answer the remaining bench gates, so nobody has to
'' watch a screen or read a task manager. One run, three answers:
''
''   Gate 4 (loss / throughput) — every stream line carries a monotonically
''     increasing sequence number, so loss is ARITHMETIC, not opinion. A dropped
''     chunk leaves a gap; a duplicate leaves a repeat. Neither can hide the way
''     a corrupted bitmap can (a bitmap missing a chunk still looks like one).
''   Gate 5 (write-under-load) — any key pressed while the PLOT window has focus
''     is echoed back as `KEY <code> AT <seq>`. Paired with the host's timestamped
''     TX entry for that keystroke, that yields a MEASURED round-trip while the
''     stream is saturated — the canary for the Windows synchronous-handle
''     hazard, where a write can block behind a read.
''   Gate 6 (cost) — nothing needed here; the app samples its own CPU.
''
'' A PLOT window is redrawn continuously so the render path is under load while
'' the stream stays verifiable — the [#30] condition, where data loss appeared
'' under render pressure rather than on a quiet line.
''
'' Ends on its own with DEBUG_END_SESSION: no --timeout needed, and no human
'' judgement about when the run is finished.
''
'' NOTE (gate 5): the PLOT window must have FOCUS for PC_KEY to see keypresses,
'' and the host latch auto-clears after ~100ms — so click the window once, then
'' type. A run with no keys pressed is still valid; it just leaves gate 5
'' unmeasured.
'' =============================================================================
CON
  _clkfreq = 200_000_000

  ' 20k lines ~= 380 KB, ~2.5s of wire time — long enough to measure a SUSTAINED rate
  ' (several 5s CPU windows still land) but short enough that the app is not left chewing a
  ' backlog. The first run of this program used 200_000: the P2 finished streaming in 22s and
  ' the app then spent ~4 MINUTES catching up with a frozen UI. A stress program should load
  ' the path, not bury it — the backlog measures nothing the first few seconds do not.
  LINES      = 20_000
  DRAW_EVERY = 250              ' redraw the plot every N lines (render pressure)

VAR
  long key                      ' PC_KEY writes the keypress here (must be hub RAM)

PUB main() | seq

  ' Proven-good directive syntax — the same shapes the fig-05 gauge emits.
  debug(`PLOT Stress SIZE 400 400 BACKCOLOR WHITE UPDATE)
  debug(`Stress ORIGIN 200 200)

  seq := 0

  repeat while seq < LINES

    ' ---- the verifiable stream -------------------------------------------
    debug("SEQ ", udec_(seq))

    ' ---- render load ------------------------------------------------------
    if seq +// DRAW_EVERY == 0
      debug(`Stress CLEAR)
      debug(`Stress GRAY 3 SET 0 0 CIRCLE 360 2 255)
      debug(`Stress RED 12 SET 0 0 LINE 160 60 5 255)
      debug(`Stress UPDATE)

      ' PC_KEY must be LAST in its DEBUG statement. Polled here rather than every
      ' line because the host latch holds a key ~100ms — far longer than a pass.
      debug(`Stress PC_KEY(@key))
      if key <> 0
        debug("KEY ", udec_(key), " AT ", udec_(seq))
        key := 0

    seq++

  debug("STRESS COMPLETE ", udec_(seq), " lines")
  debug("DEBUG_END_SESSION")
